import json
from pathlib import Path
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
p=json.loads((root/'content/base-reviewed.json').read_text(encoding='utf8'));by={x['id']:x for x in p};changes=[]
def edit(ids,meaning,connection,notes,takeaway):
 for ident in ids.split():
  x=by[ident];x.update(meaning=meaning,connection=connection,nuance=notes,keyTakeaway=takeaway)
  for q in x['quiz']:q['ruleHint']=connection
  changes.append(ident)
edit('g1-l1-wa-desu genki1-wa-desu','Identify or describe a topic: as for X, it is Y.','X は noun です; noun です is enough when the topic is understood.','は is written は but pronounced わ when it marks the topic. It tells the listener what the statement is about; it is not simply a replacement for the English subject. If both speakers already know the topic, omit it: 学生です can mean I am a student, she is a student, or they are students, depending on context. Japanese nouns generally do not require a/an and do not automatically change for plural number. です makes the noun predicate polite and non-past; it does not change with person or number. Do not append です to an ordinary finite verb such as 行くです. Adjectives have their own patterns, introduced later: 静かです and 高いです, but not 静かだです or 高いだです. Use a name/title rather than repeatedly translating English you as あなた.','State the topic only when needed; put the identifying noun before polite です.')
edit('skm3-u3-ni-kagiru','Nothing beats X; X is the best choice or best thing to do.','N / V dictionary / Vない + に限る','This pattern expresses the speaker\'s subjective recommendation, not an objectively proven ranking. A negative verb recommends avoiding an action: 無理をしないに限る means it is best not to overdo things. やっぱり often accompanies a confident preference. Do not confuse this with Nに限り (restricted to N), Nに限って (specifically/on this occasion, among other uses), or とは限らない (not necessarily). The dictionary verb 限る means to limit; the whole grammar pattern has the best-choice meaning here.','に限る = the best choice; に限り = limited to. Read the whole pattern.')
edit('g1-l1-question-ka','Make a polite question with sentence-final か.','Nですか; question word + ですか','Add か to a polite statement without English-style inversion. For an information question, keep the unknown item in its usual position: 専攻は何ですか asks what the major is. Answer with the requested information; はい/いいえ alone fits a yes/no question, not what/when. Known topics may be omitted. 何 is なん immediately before です; other contexts have なに or fixed readings. Japanese polite questions commonly end in 。 rather than requiring ?.','Statement + か asks a question; a question word asks for specific information.')
edit('g2-l21-aida-vs-aidani skm3-u1-aida-vs-aidani','間 presents a span during which something continues; 間に locates an event or change within that span.','V dictionary/ている, い-adjective, な-adjectiveな, Nの + 間 / 間に','With 間, the second situation holds or an activity continues through the relevant period. With 間に, an event, completion or change occurs within the period. It need not be a mathematical instant or only one occurrence: several errands can be completed during someone\'s absence. Compare 寝ている間、雨が降っていた (rain during the sleep period) and 寝ている間に、雨がやんだ (rain stopped during it). Choose from the intended time relation, not just the English word while.','間: ongoing within the span. 間に: an event/change within the span.')
edit('gp-dake-shika','だけ limits the amount or scope; しか…ない excludes alternatives.','Nだけ; Nしか + negative predicate; にだけ / にしか, でだけ / でしか','だけ can occur with either an affirmative or negative predicate. In the basic exclusion pattern, しか pairs with a negative predicate: 千円しかありません. It often implies that the amount is small, but dissatisfaction or insufficiency is not required: あなたにしかできない can praise someone\'s unique ability. しか normally replaces を/が, while particles such as に/で remain before it. Compare 読むだけ (only read) with 読むしかない (have no choice but to read).','しか requires a negative predicate in this construction; it does not always express dissatisfaction.')
edit('skm3-u6-tatoe-temo','Even if X is assumed, Y still holds.','たとえ + Vても / adjectiveくても / Nでも / な-adjectiveでも','たとえ explicitly introduces a hypothetical concession. The result may be a fact, judgment, intention or recommendation; it need not be the speaker\'s unshakable resolve. Distinguish a hypothetical case from a known fact, although ても itself can be used with either.','The assumed condition does not overturn the conclusion.')
edit('skm3-u10-ni-yoru-to','According to a source of information.','Source noun + によると / によれば','Names the source rather than claiming direct personal observation. It commonly accompanies そうだ, らしい, ということだ and similar reporting endings, but these are not mandatory in every report. Distinguish によって for means, cause, agency or variation.','Identify the information source; reported-style endings are common, not obligatory.')
edit('g2-l19-sonkeigo-verbs','Honorific verbs show respect toward the person whose action is described.','Honorific verb; お + suitable verb stem + になる; some verbs have special forms','尊敬語 raises the person performing the action; use it for the person being respected, not to elevate your own ordinary actions. It is not restricted to bosses or older people: customers and other people outside your group can be honored. In business speech to an outsider, your own colleague or manager belongs to your group, so do not automatically honor that manager. Special verbs such as いらっしゃる and おっしゃる have special polite forms: いらっしゃいます and おっしゃいます.','Choose the viewpoint and social relation first; honorific language respects the actor.')
edit('skm2-c13-ni-kakete-wa','Be particularly good at something; as regards a particular skill or field.','N + にかけては','Usually introduces a positive assessment of ability in a particular field. It often emphasizes confidence or comparative strength, but does not by itself prove that someone is the best in the world. Contrast neutral について, which simply marks a topic, and にとって, which marks an evaluating viewpoint.','Name a field, then evaluate strength in it.')
edit('skm2-c20-sue-ni-deliberation','After a process, finally reach a result.','Vた / Nの + 末に; Vた末のN','末に looks back over a process such as consideration, discussion or effort before its outcome. The result can be favorable or unfavorable. あげく also presents an eventual outcome but commonly highlights an unwanted or disappointing one; do not turn this tendency into a rule that every outcome must be disastrous.','末に emphasizes the preceding process; its outcome need not be negative.')
edit('skm2-c8-ni-motozuite gp-motoni-motozuite','に基づいて: based on a foundation or grounds. をもとに: using something as a basis or material.','Nに基づいて / Nに基づくN; Nをもとに / NをもとにしたN','に基づいて presents the basis for a decision, action or account, often evidence, rules, experience or principles. It is not limited to laws/science and does not guarantee literal compliance with every detail. をもとに can introduce source material used to develop something, such as an account or design. The meanings overlap; creative freedom versus strict compliance is a useful tendency, not an absolute boundary.','基づく emphasizes grounds; をもとに emphasizes a source/basis. Context determines the contrast.')
edit('skm2-c8-ni-sotte','Follow a route, plan, policy or guiding line.','Nに沿って / Nに沿うN / Nに沿ったN','Can be physical (川に沿って歩く) or abstract (方針に沿って進める). It presents something as following a guiding line, not necessarily as following every detail with no deviation. Distinguish に基づいて, which identifies a basis or grounds.','沿う = follow along; the guiding line can be physical or abstract.')
edit('skm2-c15-wo-nuki-ni-shite-wa','Without including or considering X, the stated result is not possible.','Nを抜きにしては / N抜きでは + negative result','Marks something as necessary for a result, discussion or judgment. It may express appreciation for support, but is not restricted to praise or acceptance speeches. Contrast を抜きにして without は, which can simply describe leaving something out, as in 冗談を抜きにして話す.','The は construction commonly sets a necessary condition; omission alone need not express gratitude.')
edit('skm2-p2-mai','Negative intention (will not) or negative conjecture (probably will not).','V dictionary + まい; ru-verbs also stem + まい; するまい / すまい / しまい; 来るまい / 来まい','This relatively formal form can state a decision not to do something or a belief that something will not occur. Determine the sense from the subject and context. A first-person agent often supports intention; it is not automatically a promise never to act again. ではあるまいか instead suggests a possibility: could it not be…?','まい is negative intention or conjecture; ではあるまいか is a different rhetorical pattern.')
edit('gp-ippouda skm2-c2-ippou-da-bakari-da','A change continues in one direction.','V dictionary + 一方だ / ばかりだ','Use with a predicate that describes change, such as 増える, 減る or 悪くなる. It does not necessarily mean that the rate accelerates. 一方だ can describe favorable or unfavorable trends; ばかりだ often presents an unwelcome trend. Other constructions with 一方 or ばかり have other meanings, so do not apply this restriction to every occurrence of the word.','Continuing in one direction does not mean accelerating.')
edit('skm3-u2-tabini-repetition','Each time X occurs, Y happens.','V dictionary / Nの + たびに','Highlights a recurring connection between a trigger and a result. The result need not be emotional or surprising. Use this pattern for the recurring relation you wish to assert, rather than treating it as proof that no conceivable exception exists.','たびに links repeated occasions with their recurring result.')
# Correct remaining especially misleading summaries without deleting valid attachment restrictions.
by['g1-l5-suki-kirai']['keyTakeaway']='Basic pattern: Nが好き／嫌いです. When nominalizing an action, keep its object: 本を読むのが好きです.'
by['g2-l14-hoshii']['keyTakeaway']='Basic desire for a thing: Nがほしい. Desire to perform an action: V-stem + たい.'
by['skm3-u10-rashii']['nuance']='らしい has an inference/report sense and a typical-quality sense. In 春らしい, it means characteristic of spring. っぽい describes a perceived resemblance or tendency, often informally; it is not inherently shallow or fake. Determine the sense of らしい from what precedes it and the context.'
by['gp-nagara']['nuance']='For simultaneous actions, the same person performs both, and the final predicate presents the main action: 音楽を聞きながら勉強する. Do not use this simultaneous-action pattern for separate actors. Concessive ながら, as in 知っていながら, is a different use meaning although.'
(root/'content/base-reviewed.json').write_text(json.dumps(p,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n',encoding='utf8')
(root/'audit/editorial-review-2026-11-17.json').write_text(json.dumps({'updatedCards':changes,'directSourcePages':['Genki I printed 41–42 / PDF49–50','SKM N3 printed22–23 / PDF45–46'],'scope':'Targeted editorial review; not every textbook page checked.'},ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf8')
print(len(changes),'cards expanded/corrected')
